Three-body $\tau$-decays with a missing neutrino are a key means of investigating QCD effects stemming from lepton flavor conservation's integrity. A general expression for the conserved baryonic current matrix element in these interactions is derived, and its components are fully specified in both scalar and form factor terms. A systematic method is established for determining the form factors by applying constraints from the matrix element directly, and a set of coupled second-order differential equations is derived for the form factors. This framework is universally applicable to any three-particle $\tau - \nu_\tau$ system, and when applied to $\tau \to K K \pi \nu_\tau$, the resulting form factors are shown to be in good agreement with existing experimental findings and theoretical predictions from other methods. Notably, the method yields a lower uncertainty range for form factor $A_0$, yielding $A_0^{new} = A_0^{literature} -_ {0.06}^{0.08}
